; exit 255 (see .exit)
; MM-LIFE-2h, the ARRAY form and the container maps built on it. Eight
; probes, one bit each; the arithmetic is 128+64+32+16+8+4+2+1 = 255.
;
; The array form is the third shape a counted block can carry, beside
; the record form's 47-bit reference bitmap and MM-FFI-6's foreign
; form. It is bit 15 of the shape word and it says "every payload word
; 0..count-1 is a reference", which is the only thing that can describe
; a container's element buffer - probe 64 is the proof that a bitmap
; could not have done it, because `Intern`'s table is 64 words at
; CONSTRUCTION and a bitmap holds 47.
;
;   128  a reference vector's data block reads array form and its word
;        count: 32768 (bit 15) + 16 (8 words, cap 8), and the plain
;        `vecNew`'s reads 16 - the same block, the same size, no claim
;        about its contents
;    64  `internNew`'s string vector holds 64 words, so its shape reads
;        32768 + 128 - PAST the record bitmap's 47-word capacity, in a
;        structure the standard library builds by default
;    32  the three container headers carry record maps naming their own
;        buffers: `Vec` 262152 (4 words, bit 2), `Map` 1835024 (8
;        words, bits 2/3/4), `Intern` 327688 (4 words, bits 0 and 2).
;        The `Map` is eight words and not the six it holds because
;        word 6 is the flag saying whether it owns its values - read
;        from the header rather than from the value array's shape bit,
;        which is ambiguous against an allocator with a different
;        clamp and was a SIGSEGV under the committed seed
;        (`stdlib/Mem.ax` records the measurement)
;    16  TRANSITIVE RECLAIM, four levels: freeing the vector releases
;        its data block, whose array form releases the `Str` header,
;        whose own map releases the 201-byte block behind it - and a
;        200-byte request gets that block back
;     8  THE ABLATION, in the fixture: the identical program built with
;        `vecNew` instead of `vecNewRef` does NOT get it back. Two
;        blocks come back instead of four, and the elements are exactly
;        what is lost
;     4  growth is no longer a leak: doubling a vector hands the old
;        data block to the allocator's free list, and a request of that
;        size gets it back
;     2  a `mapNewRefVals` table's value array carries the form and a
;        `mapNew` table's does not - the same one-word difference
;     1  `vecPop` zeroes what it vacates, so an element popped before
;        the free is NOT released by the array walk. Without the zero
;        the walk would find the stale handle above `len` and spend a
;        share the caller still holds - which is a use-after-free the
;        moment that block has been handed out again, not a leak
;
; TWO ABLATIONS, RUN RATHER THAN IMAGINED (2026-08-24). Both are one
; edit inside `stdlib/`, so both cost a rebuild of this fixture and
; nothing else:
;
;   `Mem.memMarkArray` made a no-op (`(| sw 32768)` -> `(| sw 0)`), so
;   no block ever carries the array form: this fixture answers 45,
;   losing 128, 64, 16 and 2 - the two shape probes, the transitive
;   reclaim, and the map-table shape. 32, 8, 4 and 1 survive, and they
;   are exactly the four that do not depend on the form: the record
;   maps on the headers, the ablation arm (which IS the unfixed
;   behaviour), the leaf release on growth, and the pop.
;
;   `vecPop`'s `(memSetWord (vecData v) (- len 1) 0)` deleted: this
;   fixture answers 254. Only probe 1 falls, and it falls to exactly
;   the count the double release predicts.
(import Mem)

(import Vec)

(import Map)

(import Intern)

(import Str)

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

(:: shapeOf (-> Int Int))

(fn (shapeOf h) (__load64 (- h 8) 0))

(:: countOf (-> Int Int))

(fn (countOf h) (__load64 (- h 16) 0))

; Build a one-element vector over a 200-byte string, drop every share
; of it, and answer whether the string's BYTE block came back. `refs`
; picks the constructor, and it is the only difference between probe 16
; and probe 8.
;
; 200 rather than a round handful of bytes: `strAlloc 200` asks the
; allocator for 201, which pads to 208, a size class nothing else in
; this fixture touches. The lesson `359-arc-str-bytes.ax` records -
; a probe sharing a size class with the loop measures the free list
; rather than what it meant to - applies in reverse here, where the
; free list IS the measurement.
(:: bytesComeBack (-> Bool Bool))

(fn (bytesComeBack refs)
  (let ((s (strAlloc 200)))
    (let (
      (bytes (memGetWord
        (cast Int s)        2
      ))
      (v (if refs
        vecNewRef
        vecNew
      ))
    )
      {
        (vecPush v s)
        (__release
          (cast Int s)
        )
        (vecFree v)
        (== (memAlloc 200) bytes)
      }
    )
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (vr vecNewRef)
    (vp vecNew)
    (it internNew)
    (mr mapNewRefVals)
    (mp mapNew)
    (mut acc 0)
  )
    {
      (set acc (+ acc (* 128 (b2i (&& (== (shapeOf (memGetWord vr 2)) 32784) (== (shapeOf (memGetWord vp 2)) 16))))))
      (set acc (+ acc (* 64 (b2i (== (shapeOf (memGetWord (memGetWord it 0) 2)) 32896)))))
      (set acc (+ acc (* 32 (b2i (&& (== (shapeOf vr) 262152) (&& (== (shapeOf mp) 1835024) (== (shapeOf it) 327688)))))))
      (set acc (+ acc (* 16 (b2i (bytesComeBack true)))))
      (set acc (+ acc (* 8 (b2i (== (b2i (bytesComeBack false)) 0)))))
      ; Cap 8 means a 64-byte data block. Nine pushes double it, the
      ; 64-byte block is filed, and a 64-byte request pops it. The
      ; elements are zeroes, which release skips as immediates, so this
      ; probe measures the block and nothing else.
      (let ((g vecNewRef))
        (let ((old (memGetWord g 2)))
          {
            (vecPushN g 9)
            (set acc (+ acc (* 4 (b2i (== (memAlloc 64) old)))))
          }
        )
      )
      (set acc (+ acc (* 2 (b2i (&& (== (shapeOf (memGetWord mr 3)) 32784) (== (shapeOf (memGetWord mp 3)) 16))))))
      ; The popped element keeps the caller's share: two retains put its
      ; count at 3, the pop zeroes the word, and the free walks past a
      ; zero rather than spending a share. Without `vecPop`'s zero the
      ; count reads 2 here.
      (let ((k (strDup "pop")))
        (let ((pv vecNewRef))
          {
            (vecPush pv k)
            (__retain
              (cast Int k)
            )
            (vecPop pv)
            (vecFree pv)
            (set acc (+
              acc              (b2i
                (==
                  (countOf
                    (cast Int k)
                  )                  3
                )
              )
            ))
          }
        )
      )
      acc
    }
  )
)

(:: vecPushN (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (vecPushN v n)
  (if (<= n 0)
    v
    {
      (vecPush v 0)
      (vecPushN v (- n 1))
    }
  )
)
